avformat/hls: read a VOD subtitle playlist along the media's segment

The subtitle renditions are read in the same call as the media. A VOD
subtitle playlist was read from its first segment on, as fast as the
reader asked, which on a transcoding server (Plex, Jellyfin) is wrong
twice over:
- a transcode started at an offset lists hundreds of empty segments
  before it, and the subtitle playlist asked for every one of them before
  its first cue (the media had skipped them already);
- a transcoder holds a request for a segment ahead of its progress until
  it gets there, and a held subtitle request held the media.

A VOD subtitle playlist now starts at the media's segment when it is
placed (opened, selected again, after a seek): the segment holding the
start of the media's previous one, the two playlists matched by their
segments' start times, as both servers cut them alike. After that it
reads on in order, also behind the media (the read-ahead is still to be
played), but never asks for a segment starting more than half a target
duration past the media's: it returns EOF for the call and is read again
with the next one. A media playlist read to its end lets every segment
through.

A VOD subtitle segment that fails (its request gives up after 2 s, 0079)
is asked for once more (Jellyfin extracts a subtitle on its first
request), then the next one, each a target duration later but 4 s at
most (Jellyfin's subtitle segments last 30 s, and its first extraction of
a long film's subtitles takes seconds); the live tolerance
(live_reload_timeout) ends the track, as for a live playlist. So a
subtitle server that stalls holds the media 2 s at most once in 4 s or a
target duration, whichever is shorter, and for that tolerance at most.

subtitle_follow_media (default 1) turns it off; a player probes it to
know the pack reads VOD subtitle renditions so.

Our own patch.

